You'll see three sections:

1. A `<script>` block at the top with snippets of JavaScript and what look like inputs
2. HTML markup in the middle with some braces and tags you might not recognize
3. A `<style>` block at the bottom filled with CSS

This three-part structure is the anatomy of every Svelte component. The script handles logic and data. The markup defines the structure. The styles control the appearance.

Now let's build our own.

## Part 2: Build a Blockquote Component

Every news story has quotes. Someone said something, and you want to set it apart from the body text. Let's build a component that does this elegantly.

But first, let's start fresh with a new project. Rather than building on top of the site you customized last week, we'll create a clean slate for experimenting with components.

### Create a new project from the template

Open your browser and navigate to <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

Click the green "Use this template" button near the top right of the page. Select "Create a new repository" from the dropdown menu.

Name this repository `components-practice`. Make sure "Public" is selected, then click "Create repository."

### Clone and set up the project

Once your new repository is created, open Visual Studio Code. Click on the "Source Control" icon in the left sidebar and select "Clone Repository."

Copy the URL of your new `components-practice` repository from GitHub and paste it into the popup. Press Enter, then select your `Code` folder as the destination. When prompted, click "Open" to open the project.

Now open a terminal in VS Code (Terminal → New Terminal) and install the project dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Then start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Open your browser to `http://localhost:5173` to see the template running. Keep this server running throughout the class—you'll see your changes appear in real time as we build components.

### Create the component file

In VS Code's Explorer sidebar, right-click on the `src/lib/components/` folder and select "New File." Name it `Blockquote.svelte`.

The file will open in the editor, completely empty. Let's fill it in.

### Start with the markup

We'll build this component step by step, starting with the simplest possible version.

Type the following into your new file:

```svelte
<blockquote>
  <p>"This is a placeholder quote that will be replaced with real text."</p>
  <cite>— Someone Famous</cite>
</blockquote>
```

Save the file. This is valid Svelte, but it's not very useful yet. The text is hardcoded, so every quote would say the same thing.

### Add props

Props (short for properties) are how you pass data into a component. Think of them as the ingredients in our recipe card. The recipe stays the same, but you can swap in different ingredients each time.

In Svelte 5, we define props using the `$props()` function. Add a script block at the top of your file:

```svelte
<script>
  let { quote, attribution } = $props();
</script>

<blockquote>
  <p>"{quote}"</p>
  <cite>— {attribution}</cite>
</blockquote>
```

The curly braces `{}` in the markup tell Svelte to insert the value of a JavaScript variable. Now our component can display any quote we pass to it.

### Add scoped styles

Let's make our blockquote look distinctive. Add a style block at the bottom of the file:

```svelte
<script>
  let { quote, attribution } = $props();
</script>

<blockquote>
  <p>"{quote}"</p>
  <cite>— {attribution}</cite>
</blockquote>

<style>
  blockquote {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 2rem;
    border-left: 4px solid #f47920;
    background-color: #f5f5f5;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem 0;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #333;
  }

  cite {
    display: block;
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #666;
  }
</style>
```

Here's something important: these styles are **scoped**. That means they only apply to elements inside this component. The `p` styles won't affect paragraphs elsewhere on your page. The `blockquote` styles won't conflict with other blockquotes that might exist in your project.

Scoped styles are one of Svelte's best features. They let you write simple, readable CSS without worrying about naming collisions or specificity wars.

### Use the component on your page

Now let's see it in action. Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` in your editor.

At the top of the file, inside the existing `<script>` block, add an import statement:

```js
import Blockquote from '$lib/components/Blockquote.svelte';
```

The `$lib` path is a special alias in SvelteKit that points to your `src/lib` folder. It saves you from writing long relative paths like `../../lib/components/`.

Now scroll down to the main content area of the page, somewhere after the headline and lead paragraph. Add your new component:

```svelte
<Blockquote
  quote="The best journalism is built on facts, context, and a willingness to keep asking questions."
  attribution="Someone who knows things"
/>
```

Save the file and check your browser. You should see a styled quote box with the orange left border and gray background.

Try adding another one with different text:

```svelte
<Blockquote
  quote="Components are like LEGO bricks for the web."
  attribution="A developer, probably"
/>
```

Notice how both quotes have identical styling, but different content. That's the power of components. Define once, use everywhere.

### Experiment with the styling

Take a few minutes to customize your blockquote. Try changing the border color, the background, the font size. Each change you make will update every blockquote on the page.

If you want to get creative, ask Copilot for suggestions. Open the chat panel and try something like: "How could I make this blockquote component more visually interesting?"

## Part 3: Build a Big Number Component

Data journalism often needs to highlight key statistics. "500,000 people displaced." "A 47% increase." "$2.3 billion in losses." These numbers deserve special treatment.

Let's build a component that makes statistics stand out.

### Create the component file

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/BigNumber.svelte`. Start with this code:

```svelte
<script>
  let { number, label, context } = $props();
</script>

<div class="big-number">
  <span class="number">{number}</span>
  <span class="label">{label}</span>
  {#if context}
    <span class="context">{context}</span>
  {/if}
</div>

<style>
  .big-number {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background-color: #1a1a1a;
    color: white;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .number {
    display: block;
    font-size: 3rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #f47920;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .context {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #999;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
```

### Understanding optional props

Notice the `{#if context}` block in the markup. This is Svelte's conditional rendering syntax. It means "only render this part if `context` has a value."

This makes `context` an optional prop. You can use the component with or without it:

```svelte
<!-- With context -->
<BigNumber
  number="47%"
  label="Increase in ridership"
  context="Compared to pre-pandemic levels"
/>

<!-- Without context -->
<BigNumber
  number="$2.3B"
  label="Total investment"
/>
```

Both are valid. The first shows three lines, the second shows two. The component adapts.

### Add it to your page

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` again. Add another import at the top:

```js
import BigNumber from '$lib/components/BigNumber.svelte';
```

Then add a few big numbers to your page:

```svelte
<BigNumber
  number="1.2M"
  label="Students enrolled"
  context="Across all CUNY campuses"
/>

<BigNumber
  number="25"
  label="Colleges and schools"
/>

<BigNumber
  number="$7,680"
  label="Annual in-state tuition"
  context="For full-time undergraduates"
/>
```

Save and check your browser. You should see three dark boxes with orange numbers, stacked vertically.

They look good, but they'd look better side by side. That's what we'll tackle next.

## Part 4: Build a Row Component

So far, our components have been about displaying content. But components can also be about layout. Let's build a component that arranges other components in a horizontal row.

### Understanding children

Some components wrap other components. A navigation bar wraps menu items. A card grid wraps cards. A row wraps... whatever you put in it.

In Svelte 5, wrapped content is called "children." When you write:

```svelte
<Row>
  <BigNumber number="100" label="First" />
  <BigNumber number="200" label="Second" />
</Row>
```

The two BigNumber components are the "children" of the Row component. Row needs a way to receive and render them.

### Create the Row component

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/Row.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { children, gap = '1rem' } = $props();
</script>

<div class="row" style="gap: {gap};">
  {@render children()}
</div>

<style>
  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .row > :global(*) {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 200px;
    max-width: 300px;
  }
</style>
```

Let's break this down:

**`let { children, gap = '1rem' } = $props();`** - We're accepting two props. `children` is a special prop that contains whatever is placed between the opening and closing tags. `gap` has a default value of `'1rem'`, making it optional.

**`{@render children()}`** - This is how Svelte 5 renders children. It's a function call that outputs whatever was passed in.

**`style="gap: {gap};"`** - We're using the `gap` prop to set the CSS gap property inline. This lets users customize spacing.

**`:global(*)`** - The `:global()` modifier tells Svelte to apply styles to child elements even though they're technically outside this component's scope. Without it, the flex styling wouldn't reach the BigNumber components inside.

### Use the Row component

Back in `src/routes/+page.svelte`, add the import:

```js
import Row from '$lib/components/Row.svelte';
```

Now wrap your BigNumber components in a Row:

```svelte
<Row>
  <BigNumber
    number="1.2M"
    label="Students enrolled"
    context="Across all CUNY campuses"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number="25"
    label="Colleges and schools"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number="$7,680"
    label="Annual in-state tuition"
    context="For full-time undergraduates"
  />
</Row>
```

Save and check your browser. The three stat boxes should now sit side by side in a neat row.

Try resizing your browser window. Notice how the boxes wrap to a new line when the window gets too narrow. That's the `flex-wrap: wrap` doing its job, making our row responsive without any extra code.

### Customize the gap

The Row component accepts a `gap` prop. Try it:

```svelte
<Row gap="2rem">
  <!-- BigNumber components here -->
</Row>
```

The spacing between items increases. This kind of customization makes components flexible enough to use in different contexts.

### Nesting and composition

This pattern of components containing other components is called composition. It's one of the most powerful ideas in component-based development.

You can nest components as deep as you want. A Page might contain Sections. A Section might contain Rows. A Row might contain Cards. Each level handles one concern, making the code easier to understand and modify.

This is how real newsroom projects are built. The Reuters graphics kit, The Pudding's templates, the New York Times' interactives—they're all composed of small, focused components that snap together like building blocks.

## Homework Assignments

### Task 1: Build three original components

Invent and build three components that could be useful in a news story. They should be different from the ones we built in class.

Here are some ideas to get you started:

- **An author bio box** with photo, name, title, and social links
 **A "key takeaways" box** that lists bullet points in a highlighted container-
 **A "read more" card** that links out to another story with a thumbnail and headline
- **A correction or update box** for editor's notes
- **A data source attribution** that credits where numbers came from
-
You're not limited to these ideas. Be creative. Think about elements you see in news stories that could benefit from consistent styling.

### Task 2: Document your components

For each component you build, write a brief explanation in your project's README.md:

- What does the component do?
- What props does it accept?
- Why did you make the design choices you did?

This documentation practice is valuable. Real component libraries include documentation so other developers know how to use them.

### Task 3: Create a showcase page

Use your three new components on a page alongside the Blockquote, BigNumber, and Row components we built in class. The page should demonstrate all six components working together.

It doesn't need to be a real news story. It can be placeholder content. The goal is to show the components in context.

Deploy your page to GitHub Pages and send me the link before next Monday's class.

### Task 4: Prepare to present

Pick one of your original components to present in class next week. Be prepared to:

- Show the component on your live page
- Walk through the code
- Explain why you designed it the way you did
- Discuss any challenges you encountered
